<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Musavat</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Musavat Party</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Musavat, meaning 'Equality', is an Azerbaijani political party founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baku">Baku</a> in 1911, which led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a> Democratic Republic of 1918–1920 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mammad-amin-rasulzade">Mammad Amin Rasulzade</a>. Banned after the Soviet takeover in 1920, it continued in exile and was revived in Azerbaijan in 1989–1992, as the country regained independence.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/musavat.jpg" alt="Musavat" width="320" height="421" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tohaomg · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Logo_of_M%C3%BCsavat_Party.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
